### List.of() – truly immutable (Java 9+)

List.of() creates a completely immutable list. The returned list does not allow add, remove, set, or any other structural modification. It also rejects null elements at creation time.

```java
List<String> accounts = List.of("SAV2001", "CUR3001", "LOAN5001");
// accounts.add("NEW");  // throws UnsupportedOperationException
// accounts.set(0, "X"); // throws UnsupportedOperationException
// List.of("SAV2001", null) → throws NullPointerException at creation
```

The list returned by List.of() is not an ArrayList. It is an internal implementation that stores elements in a compact, space-efficient form.

### List.copyOf() – immutable copy (Java 10+)

List.copyOf() creates a new immutable list that is a snapshot of the original. Changes to the original list do not affect the copy.

```java
List<String> mutable = new ArrayList<>(List.of("SAV2001", "CUR3001"));
List<String> immutable = List.copyOf(mutable);

mutable.add("LOAN5001");  // modifies the original
System.out.println(immutable);  // [SAV2001, CUR3001] – unaffected!
System.out.println(mutable);    // [SAV2001, CUR3001, LOAN5001]
```

If the source list is already an immutable List.of(), List.copyOf() may return the same instance without copying, which is an optimization.

### Collections.unmodifiableList() – view only

Collections.unmodifiableList() wraps the original list in a read-only view. The wrapper rejects modifications, but the underlying list can still be changed directly, and those changes are visible through the wrapper.

```java
List<String> original = new ArrayList<>(List.of("SAV2001"));
List<String> view = Collections.unmodifiableList(original);

// view.add("NEW");  // throws UnsupportedOperationException
original.add("CUR3001");  // modifies the original directly
System.out.println(view);  // [SAV2001, CUR3001] – the view changed!
```

This is why `Collections.unmodifiableList` is not truly immutable – it is a read-only wrapper over a mutable list. If any code still holds a reference to the original list, it can mutate the data.

### Comparison

| Method | Truly immutable? | Null elements? | Original can modify? |
|---|---|---|---|
| `List.of(...)` | Yes | No (NPE) | N/A (no original) |
| `List.copyOf(list)` | Yes | No (NPE) | No (independent copy) |
| `Collections.unmodifiableList(list)` | No (view) | Yes | Yes (view changes) |

### How does this apply to banking code?

In a banking application, you often need to return a list of transactions or account summaries from a service method. Returning a mutable list lets the caller accidentally modify internal state.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.List;
import java.util.ArrayList;

public class AccountService {
    private final List<String> activeAccountIds = new ArrayList<>();

    // WRONG: exposes internal mutable list
    public List<String> getActiveAccounts() {
        return activeAccountIds;  // caller can add/remove!
    }

    // CORRECT: return an immutable copy
    public List<String> getActiveAccountsSafe() {
        return List.copyOf(activeAccountIds);  // caller cannot modify
    }
}
```

This is a defensive copy pattern. The safe version ensures that no external code can corrupt the service's internal data.

### What exceptions are thrown?

All three immutable approaches throw UnsupportedOperationException on structural modifications. List.of() and List.copyOf() additionally throw NullPointerException if any element is null.

```java
// UnsupportedOperationException on modification
List<String> immutable = List.of("SAV2001");
try {
    immutable.add("CUR3001");
} catch (UnsupportedOperationException e) {
    System.out.println("Cannot modify immutable list");
}

// NullPointerException on null element
try {
    List<String> bad = List.of("SAV2001", null);
} catch (NullPointerException e) {
    System.out.println("Null elements not allowed in List.of()");
}
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

- **Map.of(), Set.of()** follow the same pattern as List.of(). Map.of() takes alternating key-value pairs: `Map.of("SAV2001", account1, "CUR3001", account2)`.
- **Guava ImmutableList.Builder** provides a builder for constructing immutable lists incrementally: `ImmutableList.<String>builder().add("SAV2001").add("CUR3001").build()`.
- **Stream.toList() (Java 16+)** returns an unmodifiable list. It is similar to `List.copyOf(stream.collect(toList()))` but allows null elements.
- **Immutable lists are value-based.** Do not rely on identity (`==`); use `equals()` to compare them.
- **Thread safety:** Truly immutable lists are inherently thread-safe. You can share them across threads without synchronization.
